<section id="fitness-context">
  <h2>2. Business Context &amp; Objectives</h2>
  <p>Fitness businesses commonly monitor member revenue, subscription models, pricing, retention and engagement alongside demographic and location segments. Reviewing those measures together can help frame questions about where revenue is recorded, how memberships differ, and which activity patterns merit further investigation.</p>
  <p>This challenge dashboard is designed to explore revenue trends and membership contributions; compare subscription models, prices, discounts and churn labels; examine membership acquisition and tenure; review visit and training indicators; and compare displayed measures by gym location, member type and demographic group. It presents descriptive analyses rather than establishing causes or predicting future churn.</p>
  <p>Discounts, revenue and churn are therefore treated as analytical questions: the report contains visuals relating those fields, but a relationship shown in a dashboard is not proof that one factor caused another or that a displayed “loss” is realized cash.</p>
</section>

<section id="fitness-data">
  <h2>3. Dataset &amp; Data Structure</h2>
  <p>The supplied partial Power Query extract identifies a CSV named <code>cleaned_fitness_data.csv</code>, parsed with 29 columns, comma delimiters, encoding 1252 and <code>QuoteStyle.None</code>. This establishes the file used by that query, not the upstream source or the steps that originally created the cleaned file. The full column inventory, record count, source provenance and data-quality statistics are <strong>Not verified from available project files</strong>.</p>
  <p>The PBIX report queries reference one entity, <code>cleaned_fitness_data</code>. Visible fields used by report visuals include join date, home gym location, membership type, subscription model and price, final and adjusted price, discount rate/type, tenure days, visit frequency, access hours, time in gym, personal training hours, group lessons, drink subscriptions, sauna use, age, gender, churn risk and location coordinates. This is the set of fields observable in report queries, not a verified complete schema or a field dictionary.</p>
  <p>There is no fitness Jupyter Notebook or source-data file among the inspected project assets. The supplied project brief names Python and Jupyter Notebook, but their exact workflow and any upstream transformations are not demonstrated by the available files.</p>
</section>

<section id="fitness-power-query">
  <h2>4. Power Query Data Preparation</h2>
  <p>The partial M code supplied for the project shows the CSV import and header promotion, then a later identifier sequence. The omitted steps are not present in the supplied extract, and the PBIX archive did not expose a complete Power Query mashup for inspection.</p>
  <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`Source = Csv.Document(
    File.Contents("...cleaned_fitness_data.csv"),
    [Delimiter=",", Columns=29, Encoding=1252,
     QuoteStyle=QuoteStyle.None]
),
#"Promoted Headers" = Table.PromoteHeaders(Source)`}</code></pre>
  <p>The visible identifier transformation constructs a temporary field, changes its type, removes it, renames the existing Index column, then removes duplicate rows:</p>
  <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`#"Added Custom1" = Table.AddColumn(
    #"Renamed Columns",
    "Members ID",
    each Text.Start([home_gym_location], 3)
      & Text.From([join_date], "yyyyMMdd")
      & Text.From([Index])
),
#"Changed Type4" = Table.TransformColumnTypes(
    #"Added Custom1", {{"Members ID", type text}}
),
#"Removed Columns1" = Table.RemoveColumns(
    #"Changed Type4", {"Members ID"}
),
#"Renamed Columns1" = Table.RenameColumns(
    #"Removed Columns1", {{"Index", "Members ID"}}
),
#"Removed Duplicates" = Table.Distinct(#"Renamed Columns1")`}</code></pre>
  <p>The custom value combines the first three characters of <code>home_gym_location</code>, <code>join_date</code> formatted as <code>yyyyMMdd</code>, and <code>Index</code>. The code then removes that constructed column, renames the existing <code>Index</code> field to <code>Members ID</code>, and applies <code>Table.Distinct</code> to the resulting rows. Therefore, the visible final <code>Members ID</code> is the renamed Index field, not the constructed value.</p>
  <p>A member identifier is useful for referencing membership-level records, but this sequence does not by itself prove the field is unique, stable across refreshes or a durable business key. The distinct operation is not shown with a subset-of-columns key, so no additional deduplication rules are assumed. The omitted query steps and their data-type, missing-value or formatting treatments remain <strong>Not verified from available project files</strong>.</p>
</section>

